# Assignment 1: Written Text as Operational Data

## Goals and Approach

The goal of this assignment is to look into whether how words appear can help find differences in how authors write and also differences in how the same author writes in different books. Written text will be handled as data by turning each document into a list of words and checking how often each word shows up.

For this study I will use books from Project Gutenberg as my information sources. I will use Moby-Dick by Herman Melville and Dracula by Bram Stoker as my examples. These two books will let me compare the word-frequency styles of two authors and see if some words are used more by one author than the other.

I will prepare the text by taking out HTML parts, punctuation, numbers and common words that don't add meaning. Then I will check how often each word that is left appears and compare the common words between the two books. I will use tables and bar charts to show these differences.

The main questions I want to answer are:

1. Are there differences in how often words appear between Moby-Dick and Dracula?

2. Are there words that Herman Melville uses more than Bram Stoker or the other way around?

3. Are the differences in how wordsre used big enough to possibly tell the two authors apart?

4. If possible does a certain word appear more in one book than the other?

The findings will be based on how words appear instead of what the books are about. This will help me see if the way someone writes can give clues, about who wrote a document.

